\title{Flag Complexes and the Clique Recognition Theorem}
\author{Aristotle (Harmonic)}
\date{June 17, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The clique complex (equivalently, the Vietoris--Rips complex) of a simple graph
is the abstract simplicial complex whose faces are the finite cliques of the
graph. A simplicial complex is called \emph{flag} when every finite vertex set
whose pairs are all edges of the complex's $1$-skeleton is itself a face --- that
is, when the complex withholds no simplex that its $1$-skeleton permits. We give
a self-contained development of the basic theory of flag complexes and prove the
central structural results: (A) the clique complex of any simple graph is flag;
(B) the $1$-skeleton of a clique complex recovers exactly the edges of the
original graph; and the converse direction (D) that any flag complex equals the
clique complex of its own $1$-skeleton. Combining these yields the
\textbf{Recognition Theorem} (E): an abstract simplicial complex is flag if and
only if it equals the clique complex of its own $1$-skeleton. The development is
fully formalized and machine-checked; here we present the mathematics, the
definitions, complete proof sketches, the underlying algorithms, and a
discussion of applications to topological data analysis, distributed sensing, and
geometric group theory.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

There is a fundamental and recurring move in modern mathematics: replace a
\emph{discrete} object --- a graph --- with a \emph{geometric} one --- a
simplicial complex --- so that the tools of topology become available. The
cleanest such bridge is the \textbf{clique complex} (also called the
\textbf{flag complex} or, in the metric setting, the \textbf{Vietoris--Rips
complex}): fill in a simplex on a set of vertices exactly when those vertices
are pairwise adjacent. This construction underlies topological data analysis,
the geometry of CAT(0) cube complexes, the study of right-angled Artin and
Coxeter groups, and the homotopy theory of independence and matching complexes.

A complex built this way has a striking property: it is determined entirely by
its $1$-skeleton. No information lives above the edges; every higher face is
forced by the edges below it. Complexes with this property are exactly the
\emph{flag} complexes. The purpose of this paper is to make that statement
precise and to prove the full equivalence in both directions, working entirely
from first principles over an arbitrary (possibly infinite) vertex type.

Every definition and theorem below has been formalized and verified in a proof
assistant, so the results are stated with the exactness that formalization
demands --- in particular, careful attention to finiteness, distinctness of
vertices, and the precise membership conditions for faces.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $\alpha$ is an arbitrary type of \emph{vertices}. We write
$\mathsf{Finset}\,\alpha$ for the type of finite subsets of $\alpha$.

\subsection{Abstract simplicial complexes}

\begin{definition}[Abstract simplicial complex]\label{def:asc}
An \emph{abstract simplicial complex} (ASC) on $\alpha$ is a set
$\faces \subseteq \mathsf{Finset}\,\alpha$ of finite vertex sets, called
\emph{faces}, satisfying:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item \textbf{Downward closure.} For every $s \in \faces$ and every
        $t \subseteq s$, we have $t \in \faces$.
  \item \textbf{Singleton presence.} For every vertex $a$, if $a$ belongs to
        some face $s \in \faces$, then $\{a\} \in \faces$.
\end{enumerate}
The \emph{dimension} of a face $s$ is $|s| - 1$.
\end{definition}

The first axiom is the structural heart of the definition: a face cannot exist
without all of its sub-faces. The second is a normalization convention; it
follows from downward closure (since $\{a\} \subseteq s$) but is stated
explicitly for convenience.

\subsection{The 1-skeleton}

\begin{definition}[$1$-skeleton]\label{def:skel}
The \emph{$1$-skeleton} of an ASC $K$, written $\oneSkel K$, is the simple graph
on $\alpha$ in which distinct vertices $a$ and $b$ are adjacent precisely when
the pair $\{a,b\}$ is a face of $K$:
\[
  (\oneSkel K).\Adj\, a\, b \iff a \neq b \ \wedge\ \{a,b\} \in K.\faces .
\]
\end{definition}

This is a genuine simple graph: the adjacency relation is \emph{symmetric}
(because $\{a,b\}=\{b,a\}$) and \emph{irreflexive} (because the condition
requires $a \neq b$). Formally $\oneSkel K$ is the symmetric--irreflexive
closure of the relation $a \mapsto b \mapsto \{a,b\}\in K.\faces$.

\subsection{The clique complex of a graph}

\begin{definition}[Clique complex]\label{def:clique}
Let $G$ be a simple graph on $\alpha$. Its \emph{clique complex}
$\cliqueComplex G$ is the ASC whose faces are the finite cliques of $G$:
\[
  s \in (\cliqueComplex G).\faces \iff
  (s \text{ finite}) \ \wedge\
  \bigl(\forall a,b \in s,\ a \neq b \Rightarrow G.\Adj\, a\, b\bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:wellformed}
$\cliqueComplex G$ is a well-defined abstract simplicial complex.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Downward closure: if $s$ is a clique and $t \subseteq s$, then any distinct pair
in $t$ is a distinct pair in $s$, hence adjacent; so $t$ is a clique. Singleton
presence: a singleton $\{a\}$ is vacuously a clique, so it is always a face.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The flag property}

\begin{definition}[Flag complex]\label{def:flag}
An ASC $K$ is \emph{flag}, written $\IsFlag K$, when every finite vertex set
whose distinct pairs are all edges of the $1$-skeleton is itself a face:
\[
  \IsFlag K \iff
  \forall s,\ \bigl(\forall a,b \in s,\ a \neq b \Rightarrow
  (\oneSkel K).\Adj\, a\, b\bigr) \Rightarrow s \in K.\faces .
\]
\end{definition}

Intuitively, a flag complex never contains a ``hollow'' simplex: if the entire
edge-boundary of a potential simplex is present in the $1$-skeleton, the simplex
itself must be filled in.

\section{Main Results}

\begin{theorem}[Clique complexes are flag]\label{thm:A}
For every simple graph $G$, the complex $\cliqueComplex G$ is flag.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $s$ be a finite vertex set all of whose distinct pairs are edges of
$\oneSkel(\cliqueComplex G)$. Fix distinct $a,b \in s$. By the characterization
of the $1$-skeleton, $\{a,b\} \in (\cliqueComplex G).\faces$, which means $a,b$
are adjacent in $G$. Since $a,b$ were arbitrary distinct elements, $s$ is a
clique of $G$, hence a face. Thus $\cliqueComplex G$ is flag.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Edge fidelity]\label{thm:B}
For distinct vertices $a \neq b$ and any graph $G$,
\[
  \{a,b\} \in (\cliqueComplex G).\faces \iff G.\Adj\, a\, b .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) If $\{a,b\}$ is a face, its distinct elements are adjacent by the
clique condition. ($\Leftarrow$) If $G.\Adj\,a\,b$, the only distinct pairs in
$\{a,b\}$ are $(a,b)$ and $(b,a)$, both adjacent by symmetry, so $\{a,b\}$ is a
clique.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:roundtrip}
The $1$-skeleton of $\cliqueComplex G$ is $G$ itself:
$(\oneSkel(\cliqueComplex G)).\Adj\,a\,b \iff G.\Adj\,a\,b$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Singletons]\label{thm:C}
For any flag complex $K$ and any vertex $a$ with $\{a\}\in K.\faces$, the flag
property imposes no further constraint on $\{a\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
This is a formal triviality whose content is conceptual: singletons are always
faces, so the flag condition --- which constrains higher faces via their pairs
--- says nothing new at dimension $0$. Flagness is a condition on edges and
above, never on vertices.
\end{remark}

\begin{theorem}[Flag $\Rightarrow$ clique complex of skeleton]\label{thm:D}
If $K$ is a flag complex, then
$K.\faces = (\cliqueComplex(\oneSkel K)).\faces$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
We prove both inclusions for an arbitrary finite $s$.

\emph{($\subseteq$)} If $s \in K.\faces$ and $a,b \in s$ are distinct, then
$\{a,b\}\subseteq s$, so $\{a,b\}\in K.\faces$ by downward closure, giving
$(\oneSkel K).\Adj\,a\,b$. Hence $s$ is a clique of $\oneSkel K$.

\emph{($\supseteq$)} If $s$ is a clique of $\oneSkel K$, then all its distinct
pairs are skeleton-edges; this is exactly the antecedent of the flag property,
so $s \in K.\faces$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Skeleton congruence]\label{lem:congr}
If $K_1.\faces = K_2.\faces$ then $\oneSkel K_1 = \oneSkel K_2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The adjacency of the $1$-skeleton depends on the face set only through the
predicate $\{a,b\}\in \faces$; equal face sets give equal predicates.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Recognition Theorem]\label{thm:E}
An abstract simplicial complex $K$ is flag if and only if it equals the clique
complex of its own $1$-skeleton:
\[
  \IsFlag K \iff K.\faces = (\cliqueComplex(\oneSkel K)).\faces .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Rightarrow$) This is Theorem~\ref{thm:D}. ($\Leftarrow$) Assume
$K.\faces = (\cliqueComplex(\oneSkel K)).\faces$ and let $s$ have all distinct
pairs edges of $\oneSkel K$. Rewriting through the hypothesis, it suffices to show
$s \in (\cliqueComplex(\oneSkel K)).\faces$, which follows from
Theorem~\ref{thm:A} applied to the graph $\oneSkel K$, using
Lemma~\ref{lem:congr} to identify $\oneSkel(\cliqueComplex(\oneSkel K))$ with
$\oneSkel K$ so the pair hypothesis transfers. Hence $s \in K.\faces$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Round-Trip Picture}

The five theorems organize into two maps between graphs and complexes: the
\emph{skeleton} $K \mapsto \oneSkel K$ and the \emph{fill}
$G \mapsto \cliqueComplex G$. The results pin down both composites:
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item \textbf{Fill then Skeleton is the identity on graphs}
        (Corollary~\ref{cor:roundtrip}): $\oneSkel(\cliqueComplex G) = G$.
  \item \textbf{Skeleton then Fill is the identity on flag complexes, and only
        on them} (Theorem~\ref{thm:E}): $\cliqueComplex(\oneSkel K) = K$ iff $K$
        is flag.
\end{enumerate}
Thus $\cliqueComplex$ embeds simple graphs into abstract simplicial complexes as
exactly the flag subcategory, with $\oneSkel$ a one-sided inverse that becomes a
genuine inverse precisely on flag complexes.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Clique-complex enumeration.}
To materialize $\cliqueComplex G$ for finite $G$, enumerate all cliques by
subset-growing search: start from $\emptyset$ and repeatedly extend a clique $C$
by any vertex adjacent to all of $C$. The number of faces is exponential in
$|V|$ in the worst case (the complete graph $K_n$ yields the full $(n-1)$-simplex
with $2^n$ faces), so enumeration is output-sensitive.

\paragraph{Flagness testing.}
Since $K \subseteq \cliqueComplex(\oneSkel K)$ always holds (the $\subseteq$
direction of Theorem~\ref{thm:D} needs only downward closure), flagness reduces
to the reverse inclusion: every clique of $\oneSkel K$ must be a face of $K$. A
clique of the skeleton that is not a face is a \emph{hollow simplex}, the minimal
certificate of non-flagness.

\paragraph{$1$-skeleton extraction.}
Scan the size-$2$ faces: $V = \{a : \{a\}\in K.\faces\}$ and
$E = \{\{a,b\} : \{a,b\}\in K.\faces,\ a\neq b\}$.

\section{Applications}

\paragraph{Topological data analysis.}
The Vietoris--Rips complex $\mathrm{Rips}(X,\varepsilon)$ of a point cloud is the
clique complex of the $\varepsilon$-proximity graph. Theorem~\ref{thm:A} makes it
flag and Theorem~\ref{thm:E} makes it determined by its edges, licensing the
central efficiency of persistence pipelines: store only the proximity graph and
reconstruct higher simplices on demand with no loss of homological information.

\paragraph{Distributed sensing.}
Each sensor knows only its neighbors --- purely $1$-skeletal data. Flagness is
what makes global coverage (a topological question) answerable from local
handshakes, enabling decentralized hole-detection.

\paragraph{Geometric group theory.}
Gromov's link condition characterizes CAT(0) cube complexes via flagness of
vertex links; right-angled Artin and Coxeter groups are encoded by flag
complexes. The operative principle is the one made precise here: a combinatorial
condition on edges determines the entire high-dimensional object.

\section{Discussion}

The hypotheses are minimal: the vertex type is arbitrary and the results apply to
infinite graphs verbatim. Flagness is a dimension-$1$ condition with
dimension-$\infty$ consequences --- it quantifies only over pairs yet controls
faces of every dimension --- which is what makes flag complexes tractable. The
equivalence is sharp: the boundary of a triangle (three edges, no $2$-face) is
the smallest non-flag complex, and the hollow simplex is the precise obstruction
detected by the recognition test.

\section{Future Directions}

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
  \item \textbf{Homotopy invariance through the skeleton.} Formalize that a flag
        complex's homotopy type and homology are computable from its graph alone.
  \item \textbf{Functoriality.} Promote the round-trip to a categorical
        equivalence between simple graphs and flag complexes.
  \item \textbf{Quantitative flagness.} Measure distance-from-flag via the
        dimensions and number of hollow simplices.
  \item \textbf{Persistent flag filtrations.} Prove the Rips filtration is a
        sequence of flag complexes determined by a weighted $1$-skeleton, with a
        verified stability theorem.
  \item \textbf{Local flagness and curvature.} Capture Gromov's link condition
        formally, connecting the recognition theorem to non-positive curvature.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

The clique complex of any graph is flag and faithfully records its edges
(Theorems~\ref{thm:A},~\ref{thm:B}); conversely a complex is flag exactly when it
is the clique complex of its own $1$-skeleton (Theorems~\ref{thm:D},~\ref{thm:E}).
Together these identify flag complexes with simple graphs and explain why a
one-dimensional skeleton can dictate an arbitrarily high-dimensional shape.

\end{document}

